/math-academy-id "q-31271" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8}{x-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8]^{′}}{[x-2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4{x}^{3}-4x}{1} \\\\  & =4(2)^{3}-4(2) \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-31271/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-31271"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-314139/field-1/c9ec5d6cdf4e67c7f8bce68475bed400e56bf403f3d09b5928f78b6baa512eab" :answer/id #uuid "aa9108a3-8bc4-5bec-a30e-71cb3a81451b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314139/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "073b7737-0376-5911-8179-26929be1ccd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314139/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "700668c2-4e99-529a-ba8c-5244bb440615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314139/field-1/c9ec5d6cdf4e67c7f8bce68475bed400e56bf403f3d09b5928f78b6baa512eab" :answer-field/choices ["q-314139/field-1/c9ec5d6cdf4e67c7f8bce68475bed400e56bf403f3d09b5928f78b6baa512eab" "q-314139/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314139" :question/id #uuid "0d9082f5-09a8-5fab-a36c-a6d340884982" :question/math-academy-id "q-314139" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{3}-27}{x-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{[{x}^{3}-27]^{′}}{[x-3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-314139/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-314139"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" :answer/id #uuid "4617dc05-b7c3-54a3-8ba8-402846b15bc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer/id #uuid "fa083fa1-257f-52c1-8bdd-4e16083cfdf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5c5df6a8-9107-5e7c-a593-62f2641b4f68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a4fa7d81-a547-5b0b-86d2-58082d6e4845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "11cbad53-4033-52c7-8499-d026641bafc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-31253/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer-field/choices ["q-31253/selection/3089f9a34f7a805d8b2b2d5d51b10dc7aea027e8ec8fd8d06ad11cecf2ba9740" "q-31253/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-31253/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" "q-31253/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-31253/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-31253" :question/id #uuid "c54e2735-245d-5798-9c48-ef40ec1547e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-31253/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-31253"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336874/field-1/ed43d5224151224cedff9572a0bc31a320bd7a8ebd2aa526ca5884904c9d6cb4" :answer/id #uuid "0e3eb583-b5b8-59df-a295-060954a5b36e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336874/field-1/9700accffa78065d8053126335d2ac4e2336d0d88c76df9e6a237030f752585a" :answer/id #uuid "ebe3ac8d-a0cd-5920-8bb8-598a46e5d794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336874/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdc83a8d-2600-5334-b8c5-80bdb79fd750" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336874/field-1/ed43d5224151224cedff9572a0bc31a320bd7a8ebd2aa526ca5884904c9d6cb4" :answer-field/choices ["q-336874/field-1/ed43d5224151224cedff9572a0bc31a320bd7a8ebd2aa526ca5884904c9d6cb4" "q-336874/field-1/9700accffa78065d8053126335d2ac4e2336d0d88c76df9e6a237030f752585a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336874" :question/id #uuid "a3435a77-b21b-58be-902d-60472eebe78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-336874" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11]^{′}}{[4{x}^{3}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{15{x}^{2}+12x}{12{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1)^{2}+12(1)}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336874/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336874"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336879/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "8b16ddda-957e-55df-a822-dc5432ee7125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336879/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3353fd1-8788-5859-b1fb-4979b19ffc98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336879/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer-field/choices ["q-336879/field-1/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336879" :question/id #uuid "4a9c9c3f-7fbb-5a8d-b57d-d46d296a1a20" :question/math-academy-id "q-336879" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}-4x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{2(0)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336879/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "b1b4f08e-5ce6-5522-9abe-760276d1d95a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "19380428-ddeb-5bea-a1fd-1060af3fee38" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "2fc1ce76-ddf9-5c3c-997b-fe9820824bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "e268eb65-6b1a-5013-9363-201fca83ae3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "03f0a35e-f062-5c43-8839-ca60a8ac9986" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ceb23dd-97df-5bf4-817d-999f2b55060d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114252/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-114252/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-114252/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-114252/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-114252/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-114252/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114252" :question/id #uuid "e4290f82-8e72-5fbe-9469-7463c9848c9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114252" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{-x}-x+2{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1-x){e}^{-x}-1+4x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(x-2){e}^{-x}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0-2){e}^{0}+4}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+4}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114252/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114252"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336932/field-1/3e4f896aa04e08d21c22bace65bc585466b087b3a5a2074e9e1ca64664d7ba86" :answer/id #uuid "333fcf9c-5ec8-5552-ad22-66943ec6c9cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\ln(4))^2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186264237 :answer-field/choices ["q-336932/field-1/3e4f896aa04e08d21c22bace65bc585466b087b3a5a2074e9e1ca64664d7ba86"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336910/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "9c82f9f4-cb1e-51c7-90ff-b7bb219bd1ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336910/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "76a43d30-43ed-5cdf-90ad-be1e3e53597f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336910/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-336910/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336910" :question/id #uuid "2d129fe1-3400-5f33-9a83-b534c9ef34a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-336910" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}]}^{′}}{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[2x]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336910/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336910"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336787/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "5ee1e745-ecc0-5582-9777-1be0d16ee7d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336787/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0f0e48d-0ce7-5543-ae60-5debaebe5293" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336787/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-336787/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336787" :question/id #uuid "2e728eba-52e7-5b88-ae92-5b6b07f0dcea" :question/math-academy-id "q-336787" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}]^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{e}^{3/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =3{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{3/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-336787/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336787"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336782/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d483070b-321e-52c4-9503-66f96824a418" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336782/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "1cef9843-1327-529c-8122-51addf761c1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336782/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0e3ee9-a364-50d4-9c4e-1c649c388b05" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336782/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-336782/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-336782/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336782" :question/id #uuid "335861dc-0c2a-5fbe-8432-8398a3d7dcd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336782" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{-2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)}{2{csc}^{2}⁡(2x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}{2(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}^{2}}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\cdot 4{sin}^{2}⁡(x){cos}^{2}⁡(x)}{2{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{4{cos}^{3}⁡(x)\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}}{2\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}2{cos}^{3}⁡(x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{3}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-336782/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336782"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115523/selection/7bc54ef2cebd9c4cf6fef3072c83d13d0dfd6652d02cb9919fbcbc5497b7a408" :answer/id #uuid "f9156461-004d-5c81-9b21-9e5b9d232dad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :a